                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O%27Nan+moonshine"} O'Nan moonshine
                  %h2#applications Applications
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+geometry"}> exceptional geometry
                        ,
                        = succeed "," do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+field+theory"} exceptional field theory
                  %h2#philosophy Philosophy
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+exceptionalism"} universal exceptionalism
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#modular_arithmetic"} Modular arithmetic
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cannonball_problem"} Cannonball problem
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#leech_lattice"} Leech lattice
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#mathieu_groups"} Mathieu groups
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#24cell_and_the_binary_tetrahedral_group"} 24-cell and the binary tetrahedral group
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#third_stable_homotopy_group_of_spheres"} Third stable homotopy group of spheres
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#string_theory"} String theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            24 is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            that follows 23 and precedes 25. It occurs in modern systems of measurement for historical reasons, thanks ultimately to its having many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+factors"} prime factors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 24
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  24
                  \\,=\,
                  2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 3
          %p
            and it arises in many circumstances in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#modular_arithmetic Modular arithmetic
          %p
            Fix a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            whose squares are congruent to 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulo"} modulo
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≡
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} mod
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2 \equiv 1 (\mod n) \, .
          %p
            A necessary condition for satisfying this is for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprime+integer"} coprime
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            coprime to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            satisfies this congruence. In fact, this congruence is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi gcd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} gcd(x,n) = 1
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            divides 24.
          %h3#cannonball_problem Cannonball problem
          %p The cannonball problem asks what size square pyramids can be made by stacking spheres such that the total number of spheres is a square number. Apart from the trivial solutions involving 0 spheres or 1 sphere, the only solution is a pyramid 24 layers tall:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 0
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mn 24
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mn 70
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0^2 + 1^2 + 2^2 + \cdots + 24^2 = 70^2 \, .
          %h3#leech_lattice Leech lattice
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leech+lattice"} Leech lattice
            is a “universally optimal” packing of hyperspheres in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 24
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{24}
            originally discovered in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coding+theory"} coding theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 24
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 70
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v = (0, 1, 2, \ldots, 24, 70)
          %p has zero norm under that metric.
          %h3#mathieu_groups Mathieu groups
          %p
            The largest
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+group"} Mathieu group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 24
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{24}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steiner+system"} Steiner system
            containing 24 blocks.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 24
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{24}
            is also the automorphism group of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+Golay+code"} binary Golay code
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            which is a 12-dimensional subspace of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 24
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_2^{24}
          %p
            The binary Golay code can be used to construct the Leech lattice. Essentially, each codeword defines a point in 24-dimensional space, and we can fill in and build out this set. In more detail: Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            be a Golay codeword, scale it by a factor 2, and add either
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a vector in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 24
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}^{24}
            whose components sum to an even number, or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 4
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 + 4y
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 24
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in \mathbb{Z}^{24}
            and its components sum to an odd number.
          %h3#24cell_and_the_binary_tetrahedral_group 24-cell and the binary tetrahedral group
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/24-cell"} 24-cell
            is a four-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polytope"} regular polytope
            with 24 vertices. Interpreting these vertices as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternion"} quaternion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            under quaternion multiplication, and this group is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+tetrahedral+group"} binary tetrahedral group
          %h3#third_stable_homotopy_group_of_spheres Third stable homotopy group of spheres
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/third+stable+homotopy+group+of+spheres"} third stable homotopy group of spheres
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            of order 24.
          %h3#string_theory String theory
          %p
            Critical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string+theory"} Bosonic string theory
            requires 26
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"} dimensions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spacetime"} target spacetime
            (for vanishing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilaton"} dilaton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            itself and 24 transversal directions for its oscillations.
          %p
            This is also related to the conventional choice of normalization for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+charge"} central charge
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virasoro+algebra"} Virasoro algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo −
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mi c
                    %mn 12
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mn 3
                  %mo −
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_m, L_n] = (m - n) L_{m+n} + \frac{c}{12}(m^3 - m) \delta_{m+n,0} \, .
          %p
            Several classes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+vacua"} string theory vacua
            require the presence of exactly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/24+branes+transverse+to+K3"} 24 branes of codimension 4
            transverse to a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K3-surface"} K3-surface
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            Appearances of 24 and its factors, particularly 8 and 12, are a meta-pattern in mathematics; another such meta-pattern is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} ADE classification
          %p
            For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tesselation"} tessellating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            with regular 24-cells creates the 24-cell honeycomb, in which the centers of the 24-cells are the points of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_4
            lattice. Meanwhile, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+tetrahedral+group"} binary tetrahedral group
            corresponds to the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+correspondence"} McKay correspondence
          %p
            Families of two-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virasoro+algebra"} Virasoro algebra
            plays a key role, are among the examples falling into an ADE classification.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernoulli+number"} Bernoulli number
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+curves"} moduli space of curves
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer-Wilkerson+H-space"}> Dwyer-Wilkerson H-space
                , which would be the automorphism group of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebra"} normed division algebra
                if one could exist in 24 dimensions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+eta+function"} Dedekind eta function
          %h2#references References
          %p Blog discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/06/more_mysteries_of_the_number_2.html"} More Mysteries of the Number 24
                at the
                = succeed "-Category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2021/12/the_binary_octahedral_group.html"} The Binary Octahedral Group
                at the
                = succeed "-Category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/numbers/#24"} John Baez on the Number 24
          %p
            Discussion in relation to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leech+lattice"} Leech lattice
          %ul
            %li#Sloane80
              %p
                N. J. Sloane,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em A note on the Leech lattice as a code for the Gaussian channel
                %strong 46&amp;
                3 (1980). 270–272 (
                %a{:href => "http://neilsloane.com/doc/Me75.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Gannon06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Terry+Gannon"}> Terry Gannon
                , section 2.5.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Moonshine Beyond the Monster
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511535116"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511535116
            %li#CohnKumarMIllerRadschenkoViazovska19
              %p
                H. Cohn, A. Kumar, S. D. Miller, D. Radchenko and M. Viazovska,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Universal optimality of the
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 8
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8
                    and Leech lattices and interpolation formulas
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.05438"} arXiv:1902.05438
          %p
            Discussion in relation to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-homomorphism"} J-homomorphism
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2020/12/bernoulli_numbers_and_the_jhom.html"} Bernoulli Numbers and the J-homomorphism
              at the
              = succeed "-Category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            For an introduction to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/third+stable+homotopy+group+of+spheres"} third stable homotopy group of spheres
          %ul
            %li
              J. Baez,
              = succeed "," do
                %em This Week’s Finds in Mathematical Physics: Weeks 101 to 150
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02785"} arXiv:2303.02785
        .revisedby
